" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529430 1080 "22\\,\\text{min}" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529430 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529430 1083 #uuid "36b98352-a45c-5756-ba26-e8f13b324484" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529431 1080 "20\\,\\text{min}" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529431 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529431 1083 #uuid "40afc4ac-32fd-5122-8320-a1b94bc430b1" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529432 1080 "8\\,\\text{min}" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529432 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529432 1083 #uuid "42a5f854-c421-5906-9531-dfb338eb3d00" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529433 1080 "10\\,\\text{min}" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529433 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529433 1083 #uuid "461e25d2-998f-5231-9ad9-048327e6eb31" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529457 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529434 1071 "A heated metal body cools in a room at ${12}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Its temperature is ${64}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $1$ minute and ${49}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $4$ minutes. The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, obeys Newton's law of cooling, with $t$ measured in minutes since cooling began. To the nearest minute, how long after cooling began does the body reach ${19}^{∘}\\text{C}$?" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529434 1072 #uuid "55811d49-71e0-57b3-9d4a-d1c8d9d4f58f" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529434 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-12),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-12} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-12} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-12| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-12>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-12 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-12 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-12 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =12+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(1)=64$ and $T(4)=49$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64 & =12+Ke^{-1k} \\\\ 52 & =Ke^{-1k} \\\\ 49 & =12+Ke^{-4k} \\\\ 37 & =Ke^{-4k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{37}{52} & =\\frac{Ke^{-4k}}{Ke^{-1k}} \\\\ \\frac{37}{52} & =e^{-3k} \\\\ \\ln(\\frac{37}{52}) & =-3k \\\\ k & =- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(1)=64$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}52 & =Ke^{-1(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3})} \\\\ K & =52e^{1(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3})} \\\\ K & =52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=12+52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}e^{-(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3})t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =12+(52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}})e^{-(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3})\\cdot 1}=64 \\\\ T(4) & =12+(52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}})e^{-(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3})\\cdot 4}=49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${19}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =19 \\\\ 12+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =19 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =7 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{7}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =1+3\\frac{\\ln(\\frac{7}{52})}{\\ln(\\frac{37}{52})}≈19\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529435 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529437 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529435 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529442 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529435 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529451 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529435 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529459 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529435 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529461 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529435 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529435 1077 "selection" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529435 1078 #edb/ref 17592186529451 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529435 1079 #uuid "56bac0d5-d078-5477-b2fe-4ca3e2469c85" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529426 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529436 1071 "A heated metal body cools in a room at ${23}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Its temperature is ${110}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $2$ minutes and ${62}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $8$ minutes. The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, obeys Newton's law of cooling, with $t$ measured in minutes since cooling began. To the nearest minute, how long after cooling began does the body reach ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}$?" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529436 1072 #uuid "57bd2c5d-2ec7-5e40-ac6f-231dd6ea513e" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529436 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-23),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-23| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-23>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-23 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-23 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-23 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =23+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(2)=110$ and $T(8)=62$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}110 & =23+Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 87 & =Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 62 & =23+Ke^{-8k} \\\\ 39 & =Ke^{-8k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{39}{87} & =\\frac{Ke^{-8k}}{Ke^{-2k}} \\\\ \\frac{13}{29} & =e^{-6k} \\\\ \\ln(\\frac{13}{29}) & =-6k \\\\ k & =- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(2)=110$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}87 & =Ke^{-2(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6})} \\\\ K & =87e^{2(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6})} \\\\ K & =87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=23+87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}e^{-(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6})t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =23+(87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}})e^{-(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6})\\cdot 2}=110 \\\\ T(8) & =23+(87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}})e^{-(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6})\\cdot 8}=62\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =25 \\\\ 23+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =25 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =2 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{2}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =2+6\\frac{\\ln(\\frac{2}{87})}{\\ln(\\frac{13}{29})}≈30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529437 1080 "9\\,\\text{min}" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529437 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529437 1083 #uuid "59b59f9f-0328-5e3a-b3a0-9ae94cd9029b" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529463 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529438 1071 "A heated metal body cools in a room at ${14}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Its temperature is ${114}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $2$ minutes and ${79}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $7$ minutes. The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, obeys Newton's law of cooling, with $t$ measured in minutes since cooling began. To the nearest minute, how long after cooling began does the body reach ${32}^{∘}\\text{C}$?" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529438 1072 #uuid "6178d66f-8125-5a9f-a46c-197579fd4dfe" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529438 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-14),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-14} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-14} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-14| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-14>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-14 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-14 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-14 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =14+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(2)=114$ and $T(7)=79$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}114 & =14+Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 100 & =Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 79 & =14+Ke^{-7k} \\\\ 65 & =Ke^{-7k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{65}{100} & =\\frac{Ke^{-7k}}{Ke^{-2k}} \\\\ \\frac{13}{20} & =e^{-5k} \\\\ \\ln(\\frac{13}{20}) & =-5k \\\\ k & =- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(2)=114$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =Ke^{-2(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5})} \\\\ K & =100e^{2(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5})} \\\\ K & =100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=14+100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}e^{-(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5})t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =14+(100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}})e^{-(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5})\\cdot 2}=114 \\\\ T(7) & =14+(100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}})e^{-(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5})\\cdot 7}=79\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${32}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =32 \\\\ 14+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =32 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =18 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{18}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{18}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{18}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{18}{100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =2+5\\frac{\\ln(\\frac{9}{50})}{\\ln(\\frac{13}{20})}≈22\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529439 1080 "18\\,\\text{min}" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529439 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529439 1083 #uuid "642227aa-f6b5-5f2c-9789-1258fcc2941b" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529440 1080 "19\\,\\text{min}" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529440 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529440 1083 #uuid "64fd9fd9-a113-5826-9e9f-9bf1153c8645" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529441 1080 "14\\,\\text{min}" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529441 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529441 1083 #uuid "68bd5f21-2fc4-57db-ab21-cd5920a10ff8" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529442 1080 "11\\,\\text{min}" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529442 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529442 1083 #uuid "6cc12c7b-7d04-5703-959a-c62329b886f5" 1319413953