ius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the first measurement.*" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529446 1072 #uuid "7bca55c5-9e2a-5abf-bde2-2e93390e2953" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529446 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={12}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-12).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-12} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-12} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-12| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-12>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-12 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-12 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-12 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =12+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${59}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=59,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}59 & =12+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 59 & =12+K \\\\ K & =47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=12+47{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${51}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(2)=51,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}51 & =12+47{e}^{-2k} \\\\ 39 & =47{e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\frac{39}{47} & ={e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{47}) & =-2k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{47})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the temperature of the tea is ${26}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =26 \\\\ 12+47{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =26 \\\\ 47{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =14 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{14}{47} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{14}{47}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{14}{47}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the tea will have a temperature of ${26}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{14}{47})}{-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{47})}≈13\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute." 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529447 1080 "36\\,\\text{min}" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529447 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529447 1083 #uuid "81dae93e-910d-5d6a-839a-efb7b838187c" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529445 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529448 1071 "The water boils in a pan at a temperature of ${100}^{∘}C.$ The pan with water is then placed to a room with a temperature of ${17}^{∘}C,$ and the temperature of water decreases. After $3$ minutes, it cools to ${83}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the water to have a temperature of ${43}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the water in the pan decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the water starts cooling.*" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529448 1072 #uuid "83a0fd6e-0040-59af-810e-aa17184cf1d7" 13194139535814 17592186045443 true] [17592186529448 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Th